#
# prima-materia — the Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn, entered at the
# Flying Rolls
#
# Opened from the structure-driven entrance of SPEC §14: registered at
# pm:corpusNamed in traditions/registry-modern.ttl with no node. The work
# opened is the set of Flying Rolls, the instructional papers circulated to the
# Adepti of the Second Order, as Francis King printed them with R. A. Gilbert's
# appendix of the rolls King could not trace. Every roll the volume prints was
# read; what the volume prints besides the rolls was not harvested, and the
# work node says which parts those are.
#
# Order of the file: the works; the tradition with the three decisions; the
# practices; the capacities and states; the prerequisites; the yieldings; the
# tests of a vision and the conditions of failure; the protocols; the
# cautions; the dispute; the names.
#
# Citation. Each node names the roll, its author as printed with the
# editor's identification of the motto, and the page of the 1987 Destiny
# Books print. Page numbers are taken from the running heads and folios of
# the text layer, which run without gap to p. 288.
#

#
# The works
#

pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The Flying Rolls of the Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn, in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy"@en, "Die Flying Rolls des Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn, in Francis King (Hg.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy"@de ;
    skos:note "Read on 2026-10-23 in Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy: Golden Dawn Material by S. L. MacGregor Mathers and Others, edited and introduced by Francis King, additional material by R. A. Gilbert (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987; the imprint page says first published London: Spearman, 1971), title page and imprint in the text layer of archive.org FrancisKing-AstralProjectionRitualMagicAndAlchemyGoldenDawnMaterial. The 1971 Spearman print (archive.org astralprojection0000math) is lending-restricted and its text layer answers 401; a second upload of the same 1987 print (astral-projection-ritual-magic-and-alchemy-golden-dawn-material-by-s.l.-macgrego) carries the same title page and was not needed. The text layer has 495364 bytes, an ASCII letter ratio of 1.0 and folios running without gap to p. 288."@en ;
    skos:note "Read complete: every Flying Roll the volume prints. In the body, Flying Rolls V, I part II, II parts I to III, VI (pp. 47-61), IV, XI, XXV, XXX, XXVI, XXXIII (pp. 71-101), XVI, XVIII, XIX (pp. 105-121), XV, X (pp. 125-140), XX, XXI (pp. 145-159), XIII, XXVII (pp. 163-173) and VII (pp. 179-191); in Appendix C the Stella Matutina rewriting of Flying Roll XXV (pp. 265-268); in Appendix D the rolls Gilbert found in Colonel Colvile's copies of 1898, XXII, XXIV, XXVIII, XXIX, XXXI, XXXII, III and VIII (pp. 270-285); and King's exegetical note on the rolls in Appendix A (pp. 257-260), used for the authors and dates of issue. Not harvested, because they are not Flying Rolls or are the editors' own text: the foreword and King's four-part introduction (pp. 9-43), his prefatory notes to Parts Two, Five, Seven and Eight (pp. 65-69, 143-144, 177, 195), the Cromlech Temple papers of Parts Eight and Nine (pp. 195-256), which a schismatic side order produced after 1900, King's Appendix B on the Enochian system (pp. 261-263), Gilbert's introduction to Appendix D, read for provenance only, and Gilbert's Catalogue of Flying Rolls in Appendix E (pp. 286-288)."@en ;
    skos:note "Not printed in this volume and therefore not read: Flying Rolls IX, XII, XIV, XVII, XXIII, XXXV and XXXVI and three of the seven visions of XXXIII, which King's Appendix A says Regardie had already printed and gives the places for; and the administrative first part of Flying Roll I. The contents page lists the roll at p. 93 as XXXVI, while the part title, the running head and Appendix A give XXVI, a supplement to XII on planets and tattvas; it is cited here as XXVI. Appendix A had listed XXII, XXIV, XXVIII, XXIX, XXXI and XXXII as untraced; Appendix D prints them, so the appendix is superseded inside the same volume. A web compilation under the title The Flying Rolls on archive.org (w.-wynn-westcott-the-flying-rolls) carries no imprint and was not used."@en .

pmw:GoldenDawnCipherManuscript a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The Cipher Manuscript of the Golden Dawn"@en, "Das Chiffrenmanuskript des Golden Dawn"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run. An archive.org search on the title words returned nothing on 2026-10-23; other archives and printed transcriptions were not searched. The Flying Rolls read here do not cite it by that name; Flying Roll XVI quotes the history section of the 5=6 ritual instead."@en .

pmw:GoldenDawnKnowledgeLectures a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The Knowledge Lectures of the Outer Order of the Golden Dawn"@en, "Die Wissensvorträge des Äußeren Ordens des Golden Dawn"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run. They are printed in Regardie's collection (pmw:RegardieGoldenDawn); an archive.org search on the title words alone returned nothing. Flying Roll XVIII refers to the knowledge in the first lecture and Flying Roll XIX to the brief outlines of geomancy and astrology in the lectures of the First Order, so the lectures stand under the rolls as their prior course."@en .

pmw:RegardieGoldenDawn a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Israel Regardie, The Golden Dawn (four volumes, Chicago: Aries Press, 1937-1940)"@en, "Israel Regardie, The Golden Dawn (vier Bände, Chicago: Aries Press, 1937-1940)"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run. Reachable: archive.org carries volumes I to III of the first edition as IsraelRegardie-thegoldenDawn-vol1-1937, IsraelRegardie-TheGoldenDawn-Vol2-1938 and IsraelRegardie-TheGoldenDawn-Vol3-1939, and later one-volume editions under several identifiers; a fourth volume of the first edition was not seen in the first page of results. King's Appendix A places in it the Flying Rolls this volume omits — IX in vol. I, XVII in vol. II, XXXV in vol. III, XII, XIV, XXIII, XXXVI and three visions of XXXIII in vol. IV — so it is the next work to open for the rolls, and the place to compare XXV and XXXVI on skrying with what is entered here."@en .

#
# The tradition
#

pmt:GoldenDawn a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn"@en, "Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn"@de ;
    skos:definition "The late Victorian transmission that welded kabbalistic, grimoire and Egyptian material into a graded initiatory curriculum with examinations."@en ;
    skos:note "The syncretism is documented in its own papers, which makes it the best available record of how a modern system is assembled from older parts and then presented as one."@en ;
    skos:note "Contact route, the first of the three decisions SPEC §14 requires, kept at documented, and the route is printed in the rolls themselves. They take the power of thought forms by name from the esoteric Theosophists (kriyā śakti, Flying Roll V, p. 51), the sixth and seventh senses from Theosophy (XIX, p. 117), the magnetic aura from Blavatsky (XXII, p. 274), and recommend the Voice of the Silence and Light on the Path (XV, p. 128); they quote Lévi on the will (II, p. 57), use the odic fluid of the mesmerists (V, pp. 48-50, with the editor's note), cite the Bhagavadgītā, the Gnostic Achamoth, the Edda, the Sepher Yetzirah, the Fama and Confessio Fraternitatis, Agrippa, Barrett and Peter of Abano for the Theban letters (XXXII, p. 284), Lilly for horary astrology (XXIV, p. 277), and Bulwer Lytton's Zanoni (XXI, p. 156). Whatever here agrees with traditions/theosophy.ttl, traditions/mesmerism.ttl or the grimoire and kabbalistic holdings is reception along a printed route and counts once; traditions/thelema.ttl and traditions/typhonian.ttl stand downstream of this one."@en ;
    skos:note "Recension and edition, the second decision. The rolls exist only in copies, and the volume read is a selection: King printed the rolls Regardie had not, from copies he does not describe, and Gilbert added in 1987 the rolls King could not trace, from copies made in 1898 by Colonel Sir Henry Colvile and others and bound by Waite. Appendix C shows that the schismatic Stella Matutina rewrote at least one roll, so a roll in another collection is not to be assumed identical with the text cited here. Every node cites the 1987 Destiny Books print, the only print that could be read; the 1971 Spearman print is lending-restricted."@en ;
    skos:note "The third decision, the number of citable places, counted from the reading: twenty-eight rolls read, Flying Roll XXV in two versions; twenty-one practices; twenty-one capacities and six states the prerequisites point at; five nodes on the testing of a vision; ten further conditions of failure as their own nodes; sixteen prerequisites; nineteen yieldings; three protocols; fourteen cautions; one dispute; and five names, each at a page."@en ;
    skos:note "Two counts, kept apart as SPEC §15 asks. Signs of success named in the rolls, across all of them: twenty-four. From Flying Roll V, five: the exhaustion after a visitor that stopped at once and for good; the sick woman who grew stronger and whose mediumistic experiences ceased; the injurious effects that never returned; the fascination that ceased within months; the profane phrase driven off though not destroyed. From IV, two: the sense of change like a faint at the onset of the vision; the influx of courage in place of the expected death. From XI, five: the flashes of radiance from a tablet in flashing colours; the exhaustion felt as charge passes into a telesma; the reversal of left and right in the mirror vision; the solidity, relief and bird's-eye view of true projection; the symbol that grows until the seer seems to pass into it, or the scene opening like a drawn curtain. From XXV and its Stella Matutina version, four: the abstract idea followed by the bodily feeling and the sound of the element; the grey mist on which the symbol stands; the laws of the astral country that the seer cannot make or unmake; the seer's own figure seen, then looked out of. From XIX, three: the finer gradations of light and dark in the vault; the definite sound sent for a definite purpose; the magnet felt with closed eyes and its poles told apart. From XXXIII, two: the vision unaltered by Tau; the god strengthened by the tests. From XXVII, one: the response that answers an invocation. From XXI, one: the divine vision that almost blinds at the rare moment of contact. From XXIV, one: the recovery that came two days after the date predicted from the horary figure. Conditions of failure, stated as what shows that it did not work and why: thirteen — the deliberate experiment of the will that fails because curiosity divides it (pmc:GoldenDawnDividedWill); the wandering mind under which no beginner sees (pmc:GoldenDawnWanderingMind); the vision that changes or vanishes under Tau and so was memory (pmc:GoldenDawnMemoryFalsification); the figure that cowers before the letter and so was deceptive (pmc:GoldenDawnTestingByDivineNames, read at its negative outcome); the seer who believes he has gone elsewhere and is among the images of his own aura (pmc:GoldenDawnOwnAura); the complex symbol formed when several work in concert (pmc:GoldenDawnComplexSymbol); the symbol that carries its maker's influence or a hidden uncleanness (pmc:GoldenDawnForeignSymbol); the self-hypnotisation that yields hysterical visions (pmc:GoldenDawnSelfHypnotisation); the colour that comes out wrong in the nature opposed to one's own (pmc:GoldenDawnThoughtSelfishness); the correspondence not carried to the last detail (pmc:GoldenDawnIncompleteCorrespondence); the work talked about, or told to the person it is done for (pmc:GoldenDawnBrokenSecrecy); the transmutation attempted by chemistry alone, or published, or sought for gain (pmc:GoldenDawnChemistryAlone); and the unsuitable state of mind, entered as a prerequisite (pmc:GoldenDawnVisionPresupposesCalm). Beside the count stand two reported failures with a cause given: the woman who drifted back into passive mediumship and died (V), and the horary judgements that other choices of house would have produced and that the outcome refuted (XXIV)."@en ;
    skos:note "Open after this run, as the place a later run starts: the rolls that are in Regardie and not in this volume, the cipher manuscript and the knowledge lectures (see the work nodes); the pedigree the rolls assert, which Flying Roll XVI states by quoting the 5=6 ritual, an order existing from time immemorial and brought to Europe by C.R., born 1378 and dead 1484, together with the claim that the Fama carries a deliberate blind (pp. 105-108). The pedigree is not entered as an origination because no work standing at its beginning was read in this run."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The cipher manuscripts and knowledge lectures; Regardie, The Golden Dawn; the Flying Rolls, in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls, pmw:GoldenDawnCipherManuscript, pmw:GoldenDawnKnowledgeLectures, pmw:RegardieGoldenDawn ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "The Flying Rolls, in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy, additional material by R. A. Gilbert (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987)" .

#
# The practices
#

pmp:GoldenDawnMagicalProjection a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Magical projection: an image made by the imagination, vitalised and directed by the will"@en, "Magische Projektion: ein von der Vorstellungskraft gebildetes Bild, vom Willen belebt und gelenkt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The base operation of the first part of the rolls. To imagine is to create a form on the astral or a higher plane, as objective to beings there as the earth is to us. The will alone sends out an undirected current and the imagination alone makes an image that does nothing; the two together, the image made first and the will then using it, give the effect. Westcott's supplement separates this imagination, an orderly and intended process, from fancy, and gives it the Theosophical name kriyā śakti; Bullock's Theurgia adds that every thought takes form, that elementals join such forms and give them a life of their own, and that the force of a thought is proportionate to the intensity of the volition behind it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll V, Some Thoughts on the Imagination, by Resurgam (E. W. Berridge), with supplementary remarks by N.O.M. (W. Wynn Westcott), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 47 and 51-52", "Flying Roll XXVII, The Principia of Theurgia, by L.O. (P. W. Bullock), ibid., p. 169" .

pmp:GoldenDawnOdicInvestiture a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Closing oneself: an imagined investiture of odic fluid against a drain of vitality"@en, "Sich verschließen: eine vorgestellte Umhüllung aus odischem Fluidum gegen das Abziehen von Lebenskraft"@de ;
    skos:definition "Before admitting a visitor he judged to be feeding on his vitality, Berridge imagined himself enclosed on all sides by a covering of odic fluid that did not touch him and that no hostile current could pass. He did the same occultly for a sickly medium while seeming only to give her mesmeric passes, and for a woman troubled by a man in bad health he imagined the two facing each other, set a shield between them, enclosed her and made the invoking pentagram over her, without telling her when he would do it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll V, Some Thoughts on the Imagination, by Resurgam (E. W. Berridge), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 48-50" .

pmp:GoldenDawnSeveringOdicFilaments a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Cutting the odic threads of a fascination with an imagined sword and burning the ends"@en, "Die odischen Fäden einer Faszination mit einem vorgestellten Schwert durchtrennen und die Enden verbrennen"@de ;
    skos:definition "For a woman who could not stop thinking of a man she did not care for, and who was said to know something of voodoo, Berridge imagined the man casting out currents of odic fluid that had caught her in a net, cut them with a sword in his hand and burnt the floating ends with a torch."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll V, Some Thoughts on the Imagination, by Resurgam (E. W. Berridge), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 50" .

pmp:GoldenDawnElementalShell a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Holding the haunting phrase before one as a creature and sending an occult shell into it"@en, "Die verfolgende Redewendung als Geschöpf vor sich halten und ein okkultes Geschoss hineinsenden"@de ;
    skos:definition "A man was haunted for years by a profane expression another man had used. Berridge took the words for a mantram that had vitalised an elemental and advised him, the next time it came, to imagine the profanity embodied in a horrible creature, hold it firmly before him and send into it an occult shell that would penetrate and explode it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll V, Some Thoughts on the Imagination, by Resurgam (E. W. Berridge), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 50" .

pmp:GoldenDawnScarletRay a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "The scarlet ball of compacted force in the heart, projected on the subject"@en, "Die scharlachrote Kugel verdichteter Kraft im Herzen, auf den Gegenstand geworfen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Florence Farr's method for cultivating the will. The operator imagines the head as a centre with thoughts radiating like rays in a globe; wanting a thing is the first step; he places a distinct image of it as if in the heart, gathers every stray ray of thought upon it until it feels like one glowing scarlet ball of compacted force, and projects that force on the subject he wishes to affect. Mathers' note in Flying Roll VI inserts a step before it, the ray drawn down from Kether."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll II, part III, Three Suggestions on Will Power, by S.S.D.D. (Florence Farr), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 59-60" .

pmp:GoldenDawnKetherRay a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Drawing a ray from Kether at the crown through the path of Gimel into the heart"@en, "Einen Strahl aus Kether am Scheitel über den Pfad Gimel ins Herz ziehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mathers' supplement to Farr's exercise: before the scarlet ray is brought into intense action in the heart, the Adept raises his thought to the divine light in Kether, takes Kether as the crown of his head, and draws a ray from it into his heart, his Tiphereth, by his path of Gimel; only then does he send the scarlet ray into action, which is said to be both more powerful and safer."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll VI, Concerning Flying Roll No. II, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 61" .

pmp:GoldenDawnPentagramRitual a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "The rituals of the pentagram and the hexagram, banishing and invoking"@en, "Die Rituale des Pentagramms und des Hexagramms, bannend und anrufend"@de ;
    skos:definition "Named in the rolls and not described, because the papers that teach them precede the Second Order. Berridge says he would have used the banishing pentagram for his own protection had he been initiated at the time, and made the invoking pentagram over a woman for hers; Westcott sets mastery of the pentagram and hexagram rituals, with the first six manuscripts and the making of the implements, before the study of the Tarot; Pullen-Berry's Flying Roll VIII gives a geometrical construction of the figure."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll V, Some Thoughts on the Imagination, by Resurgam (E. W. Berridge), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 49-50", "Flying Roll XIX, The Aims and Means of Adeptship, by N.O.M. (W. Wynn Westcott), ibid., p. 120", "Flying Roll VIII, A Geometrical Way to Draw a Pentagram, ibid., Appendix D, p. 285" .

pmp:GoldenDawnSpiritVision a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Spirit vision by contemplation of a Tarot trump, alone or with one or two Adepti in the vault"@en, "Geistschau durch Betrachtung eines Tarot-Trumpfs, allein oder mit ein oder zwei Adepten im Gewölbe"@de ;
    skos:definition "The mode set out in Flying Roll IV. The operator secures an hour or more free of interruption, enters the vault or a private room, sits in silence for some minutes, performs the Qabalistic Cross and prayer, and contemplates an object such as a Tarot trump, either gazing at it until he seems to see into it or holding it to the forehead with closed eyes after studying its symbolism, colours and analogies, and then sinks into its abstract idea, indifferent to his surroundings, considering its letter, number and paths. The example is the Empress, whose letter Daleth was pronounced in vibratory manner; the seers rose through clouds to a temple, gave the signs of the Netzach grade, of the Portal and of 5=6 in thought to enter, and were led to a cup and a heart."@en ;
    skos:note "The vision may come as a reverie or with a sense of change like a faint, which the seer is told not to resist if he is highly inspired; a disturbance brings him back as from a doze, and some can end the vision by will and others at first cannot. Two signs of success, counted at the tradition instance."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll IV, An Example of Mode of Attaining to Spirit Vision, by S.S.D.D. (Florence Farr) and F. (Elaine Simpson), 10 November 1892, in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 71-73" .

pmp:GoldenDawnDescrying a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Descrying in the Spirit Vision: clairvoyance by a tattva symbol, names and the implements"@en, "Schauen in der Geistschau: Hellsehen über ein Tattva-Symbol, Namen und die Geräte"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first of Mathers' three stages. The seer takes a symbol of convenient size, best a simple or compound tattva card in ordinary rather than flashing colours, places it before him with the hands beside it or holds it up, gazes at it with full concentration while understanding its meaning and relations, then closes the eyes and keeps its form and colour in the aura as clearly as he saw them, moving the effort from the optic nerve to thought-seeing. He then sounds the highest divine names belonging to it, then the archangelic and angelic names, seeks a scene, which may open as a curtain drawn aside, looks about for objects and beings, calls them by suitable titles and grade signs and tests them by the names. Brodie-Innes adds that knowing the attributions draws thought, nerve force and blood to the matching tract of the brain, and that the vibrated names and the implement touched raise the effect until the rest of the brain is shut down. The four implements should be present and the one suited to the inquiry held; a large tablet with the divine names of the quarters may frame the symbol. Mathers' Flying Roll XXX prescribes the cards: equal areas, the long side upright, a sub-tattva of about a fifth of the main one, clean card and correct colours."@en ;
    skos:note "The rolls call the old name of the art Staying in the Spirit Vision, and the stayer is one who finds what he seeks, as against one who receives visions he cannot direct."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XI, Clairvoyance, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), issued 30 March 1893, in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 75-79 and 82", "Flying Roll XXV, Essay on Clairvoyance and Travelling in the Spirit Vision, by Sub Spe (J. W. Brodie-Innes), ibid., pp. 85-88", "Flying Roll XXX, Tattva Cards and Tattvic Clairvoyance, by D.D.C.F., ibid., pp. 91-92" .

pmp:GoldenDawnTravellingInTheSpiritVision a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Travelling in the Spirit Vision: a ray of the self sent to the place, seeing there directly"@en, "Reisen in der Geistschau: ein Strahl des Selbst an den Ort gesandt, dort unmittelbar sehend"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second stage. The Adept sends out from his ego a perceptible ray of his identity and by instructed will makes it travel to the place desired, focuses it there, sees directly and not by reflection, perceives his bodily home and re-enters it. It may start from a symbol or from will alone, but the divine names are to be used throughout. Brodie-Innes describes it from his own practice: he first becomes dimly aware of a figure walking among the scenes, recognises it as his own, learns to look through its eyes and feel with its senses, and then directs it and visits in it what he had only watched. The Stella Matutina version in Appendix C sets the same out as five numbered stages."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XI, Clairvoyance, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 79-80 and 82-83", "Flying Roll XXV, Essay on Clairvoyance and Travelling in the Spirit Vision, by Sub Spe (J. W. Brodie-Innes), ibid., pp. 88-89", "Flying Roll XXV in the Stella Matutina version, ibid., Appendix C, p. 267" .

pmp:GoldenDawnRisingInThePlanes a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Rising in the Planes: up the paths of Tau and Samekh from Malkuth toward Tiphereth"@en, "Aufsteigen in den Ebenen: die Pfade Tau und Samekh hinauf von Malkuth gegen Tiphereth"@de ;
    skos:definition "The third stage, a spiritual process with higher aims. By contemplation of the divine the operator formulates a Tree of Life reaching from himself into the realms above, pictures himself standing in Malkuth, strives upward by the divine names along the path of Tau toward Yesod, disregarding the crossing rays that draw him aside, looks up to the light from Kether, follows the path of Samekh, the arrow, to Tiphereth, invokes the Great Angel HUA, imagines himself fastened to the Cross of Suffering and vibrates the names of that position, so that the mental vision may reach higher planes. The stage of Yesod, with its confusion of reflected and coloured rays, must be passed through to go higher, and instruction is needed there."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XI, Clairvoyance, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 76 and 80-81" .

pmp:GoldenDawnPlanetaryLetters a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "The seven double letters formulated against the errors of vision: Tau, Kaph, Beth, Gimel, Daleth, Peh, Resh"@en, "Die sieben Doppelbuchstaben gegen die Irrtümer der Schau gebildet: Tau, Kaph, Beth, Gimel, Daleth, Peh, Resh"@de ;
    skos:definition "The corrective the rolls prescribe for the three sources of error, memory, imagination and actual sight. The seer formulates before the suspect image, in brilliant white light, the letter of the planet that rules the error: Tau of Saturn, the Great One of the Night of Time, against memory; Kaph of Jupiter against imagination; Beth of Mercury against lying, intellectual untruth; Gimel of the Moon against a wavering mind; Daleth of Venus against the enticement of pleasure, which Brodie-Innes gives as intellectual vanity; and, in Mathers' note, Peh of Mars against anger and violence and Resh of the Sun against haughtiness and vanity. Brodie-Innes says the letter will cause a memory picture, or the product of lying delusion, to vanish."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XI, Clairvoyance, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 81 and n. 4", "Flying Roll XXV, Essay on Clairvoyance and Travelling in the Spirit Vision, by Sub Spe (J. W. Brodie-Innes), ibid., p. 89", "Flying Roll XXV in the Stella Matutina version, ibid., Appendix C, pp. 267-268" .

pmp:GoldenDawnEnochianSquareVision a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Skrying a square of the Enochian tablets through its pyramid, god, sphinx and angel"@en, "Ein Quadrat der henochischen Tafeln über seine Pyramide, seinen Gott, seine Sphinx und seinen Engel schauen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The practice of the four visions printed from Flying Roll XXXIII. The seer vibrates the names of the lesser angle, reads the call that belongs to it, encloses himself in the pyramid of the square, which a note says is only the symbolic formula of the plane, rises through it, calls up the Egyptian god-form standing on the pyramid, gives the 5=6 signs and tests, then asks to be shown the sphinx of the square and its angel, and asks each for the force of the square in the universe, in the weather, in animals, plants and minerals and in man. Flying Roll XXXI adds that an Enochian letter placed on the square instead of the Roman one aids the skrying by holding the concentration on that square alone."@en ;
    skos:note "Two things the visions report that bear on the claim. Vigilate tried for a long time and could not get the force of her square clearly; and Berridge, scorched in the crater of a volcano, began to wrap himself in an Apas and was told by a stern voice that he must bear the discomfort if he wanted to learn. Pullen-Berry records an attribution, the elephant, that seemed incongruous to him and that he came to accept because the formulated Tau did not change the vision."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XXXIII, Visions of Squares upon the Enochian Tablets, by Vigilate, F.E.R. (Annie Horniman), Anima Pura Sit (H. Pullen-Berry) and Resurgam (E. W. Berridge), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 95-101", "Flying Roll XXXI, Correspondence between the Enochian and Ethiopic Alphabets, by V.N.R. and V.H. Ad.Maj., ibid., Appendix D, p. 282" .

pmp:GoldenDawnTelesmaConsecration a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Making a telesma or flashing tablet line by line, and consecrating it"@en, "Ein Telesma oder eine Blitztafel Linie für Linie anfertigen und weihen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Tablets and telesmata are made in flashing colours, a colour set against its complementary so that the two shine by contrast; in them the elemental forces manifest most readily. An Adept of high attainment charges the tablet with akashic force as each line and colour is added; a beginner cannot, and his finished telesma needs a ceremony of consecration, after which it is sacred to his touch alone. Any telesma is better consecrated, kept apart from contamination and from other telesmata."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XI, Clairvoyance, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 77" .

pmp:GoldenDawnSenseCultivation a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Cultivating the senses: sight in the dark vault, hearing, and touch on the forehead"@en, "Die Sinne schulen: Sehen im dunklen Gewölbe, Hören, und Tasten an der Stirn"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second aim of the Second Order, the extension of perception, begins with the bodily senses. Practising in the dark vault, the student learns the finer gradations of light and darkness, though perfect darkness is hard to get and only certain days allow it; sight is trained on contrasts of colour and the flashing colours, with view in the mirror; hearing until there is some success in clairaudience; and touch, of which the first exercise is to detect with closed eyes a magnet held near the skin, best at the forehead. Taste and smell can be developed too."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XIX, The Aims and Means of Adeptship, by N.O.M. (W. Wynn Westcott), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 116-117" .

pmp:GoldenDawnDivination a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Divination by geomancy, astrology and the Tarot, with robe, rose-cross and implements"@en, "Wahrsagung durch Geomantie, Astrologie und Tarot, mit Robe, Rosenkreuz und Geräten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Three systems are taught, geomancy and astrology in outline in the First Order and the Tarot in the Second, and all are aids by which intuition is trained; the rules of each lead the intuition and limit it to a definite end. Success needs a concentration of mind and of vital and astral force on the matter, the body at rest and no opposing force. The Adept therefore divines in the white robe, the yellow sash of his grade and the rose-cross, keeps the sword at hand against hostile and errant forces, uses the lotus wand to appeal to the zodiacal or planetary force concerned, and lays all four elemental implements before him to keep his aura balanced, taking up the one that matches a factor of the figure to weigh its strength. By these means the lower elementals are forced to abandon their attacks, and the higher self receives prompting from higher powers."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XIX, The Aims and Means of Adeptship, by N.O.M. (W. Wynn Westcott), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 120-121", "Flying Roll XXVIII, On the Value of Magic Implements and Insignia in Methods of Divination, by D.D.C.F. and N.O.M., ibid., Appendix D, pp. 279-280" .

pmp:GoldenDawnHoraryFigure a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "A horary figure drawn for the moment of the intuition to draw it, with the house of the querited counted exactly"@en, "Ein Stundenhoroskop für den Augenblick der Eingebung, es zu stellen, mit genau abgezähltem Haus des Erfragten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Berridge's lecture of 1892. On hearing by letter that his maternal uncle, over eighty, was ill with pneumonia, he drew the figure for the moment of the intuition to do so, while reading the letter, took the house of the querited by counting the siblings of his mother from the tenth, the dead among them, and timed the event by the degrees the applying significator wanted to its aspect, one week to a degree in a movable sign and a succedent house. His ten observations then show how other choices of house, of aspect and of correction for latitude would have given other judgements, each of which he says the outcome contradicted."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XXIV, part of a lecture on horary astrology delivered in 1892, by Resurgam (E. W. Berridge), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), Appendix D, pp. 276-278" .

pmp:GoldenDawnRosicrucianSalutation a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "The salutation of the Fratres: Ave Frater, Roseae Rubeae, et Aureae Crucis"@en, "Der Gruß der Fratres: Ave Frater, Roseae Rubeae, et Aureae Crucis"@de ;
    skos:definition "When brethren meet, one says Ave Frater, the second Roseae Rubeae, the first et Aureae Crucis; then, on formal occasions and between Fratres little known to each other, one says the Benedictus formula and uncovers cross or seal; on parting they say Vale with Sub umbra alarum tuarum, Jehovah. Westcott says the observance directly maintains the psychic link that has always bound the members of the Order, and is therefore more than a form."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XVI, The History of the Rosicrucian Order, by N.O.M. (W. Wynn Westcott), lecture of 17 August 1893, in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 109-110" .

pmp:GoldenDawnAdeptusMinorRitual a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "The 5=6 ceremony of the Adeptus Minor: the cross, the rose on the representative of C.R., the third point in the vault"@en, "Die 5=6-Zeremonie des Adeptus Minor: das Kreuz, die Rose am Vertreter von C.R., der dritte Punkt im Gewölbe"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mathers' Good Friday lecture of 1893 reads the ceremony as a means of moving the aspirant's consciousness from the Ruach into the Neschamah. It names three places where the exchange can begin: the aspirant fastened on the cross, with the invocation of the Angel HVA; the aspirant touching the rose on the representative of C.R. in the vault; and the third point, when he kneels and the Chief Adept calls down the white brilliance of the divine spirit. At these points, whether he understands it or not, the aspirant actually approaches his own genius."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll X, Concerning the Symbolism of Self-Sacrifice and Crucifixion contained in the 5=6 Grade, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), 31 March 1893, in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 137-140" .

pmp:GoldenDawnHermeticTransmutation a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Alchemy on the plane of Assiah: the physical process worked together with the will on the soul of things"@en, "Alchemie auf der Ebene Assiah: der physische Vorgang zusammen mit dem Willen am Seelischen der Dinge betrieben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Westcott's lecture of 1890 distinguishes alchemy on four planes: on Assiah the occult chemistry of the Adept, who adds to skill with materials the will power to act on the astral counterparts of things; on Yetzirah the creation of living forms; on Briah the works of art and genius; and on the spiritual plane the account of the soul's descent and re-ascent written in chemical language. For the first, he holds that the metals are compounds of a metallic root, the mercury of the philosophers, and sulphurs, and that a base metal purged of its sulphurs stage by stage gives each metal in turn and at last gold, by separation, by maturation of mercury, or by fusing the stone of the philosophers with a base metal, provided the work is done on the astral plane at the same time as on the physical."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll VII, Alchemy, by S.A. (W. Wynn Westcott), lecture of 1890, in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 180-181, 184 and 189-191" .

pmp:GoldenDawnTheurgia a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Theurgia: communion with planetary spirits by sound, colour, number and form, the circle drawn as a vortex"@en, "Theurgie: Verkehr mit Planetengeistern durch Klang, Farbe, Zahl und Form, der Kreis als Wirbel gezogen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Bullock defines theurgia as communion with planetary spirits, the hierarchies and the gods of light, worked through the combined forces of sound, colour, number and form with the whole attention concentrated on the plane whose signature is expressed. The rule is appeal to the divine and superhuman and conjuration of the inferior. Information about a distant being or place is had either by invoking its simulacrum or by travelling there in projection; the circle, the key of the ceremonial, is to be drawn as a true magical vortex that shuts the mind off from every outside impression; sigils are used because whatever resembles a force takes part in it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XXVII, The Principia of Theurgia or the Higher Magic, by L.O. (P. W. Bullock), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 169-172" .

#
# The capacities
#

pmc:GoldenDawnKriyaSakti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Kriyā śakti: the power of a thought form to produce perceptible results outside, when the will fertilises it"@en, "Kriyā śakti: die Kraft einer Gedankenform, äußere wahrnehmbare Wirkungen hervorzubringen, wenn der Wille sie befruchtet"@de ;
    skos:definition "Westcott's term for the capacity the first part of the rolls is about: the imagination, fertilised by the will, produces external, phenomenal and perceptible results by its own energy, and on the Hermetic dogma he cites any idea can be made to manifest outwardly once the art of concentration is gained. He adds the Qabalistic claim that man by this creative power of will and thought stands nearer the creating deity than the angels, who execute commands and do not originate."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll V, supplementary remarks by N.O.M. (W. Wynn Westcott), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 51-52" .

pmc:GoldenDawnOdicProtection a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Protection against a vampirising person, said to have held at once and for good"@en, "Schutz gegen einen aussaugenden Menschen, der sofort und dauerhaft gehalten haben soll"@de ;
    skos:definition "The outcome Berridge reports for the investiture in three cases. For himself the exhaustion he felt after every long visit stopped at once and he never had to repeat the process; the medium grew stronger, looked more human in another student's words, and her mediumistic experiences ceased; the woman troubled by a sick man never again felt the injurious effects and stayed wholly passive to him."@en ;
    skos:note "Three signs of success, each reported by the operator, and in the third case with a precaution that matters: the woman was not told when the work would be done. The same roll reports the case's failure: the medium did not follow the advice to hold herself positive, drifted back into passive mediumship, broke down and died after a lingering illness, and Berridge dreamt two weeks later of an elemental that attacked him, which he took for the same spirit in revenge."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll V, Some Thoughts on the Imagination, by Resurgam (E. W. Berridge), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 48-50" .

pmc:GoldenDawnFascinationSevered a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "A fascination broken: it ceased soon, and the acquaintance ended within months"@en, "Eine Faszination gebrochen: sie hörte bald auf, und die Bekanntschaft endete binnen Monaten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The outcome reported for the cutting of the odic threads: the unnatural fascination soon ceased, and in a few months the acquaintance between the woman and the man came to an end."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll V, Some Thoughts on the Imagination, by Resurgam (E. W. Berridge), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 50" .

pmc:GoldenDawnElementalDispersed a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "An elemental driven off but not destroyed: the haunting phrase came much less often"@en, "Ein Elemental vertrieben, aber nicht zerstört: die verfolgende Redewendung kam viel seltener"@de ;
    skos:definition "The outcome reported for the occult shell, and the only one in Flying Roll V that falls short of what was intended: the man told Berridge that he had not succeeded in disintegrating the elemental, but had driven it away and was now very little troubled by it."@en ;
    skos:note "The roll states the partial result and gives no reason for it. Counted among the signs of success, with the shortfall named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll V, Some Thoughts on the Imagination, by Resurgam (E. W. Berridge), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 50" .

pmc:GoldenDawnWillOverAnother a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Control over another's will, enough to change his natural tendencies"@en, "Gewalt über den Willen eines anderen, genug, um seine natürlichen Neigungen zu ändern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Farr reports from her own experience that the first step is very hard, to gain control of another person's will so as to alter his natural tendencies, but that once it is done the force set in motion becomes almost uncontrollable: the other seems at times to live only in the operator's presence, and his last state is worse than his first. She compares the cases of people cured by faith healers and professional hypnotists."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll II, part III, Three Suggestions on Will Power, by S.S.D.D. (Florence Farr), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 59" .

pmc:GoldenDawnClairvoyance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Clairvoyance: the inward of a symbol seen as in a mirror, with left and right reversed"@en, "Hellsehen: das Innere eines Symbols wie in einem Spiegel geschaut, mit vertauschtem Links und Rechts"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity of the first stage. Losing sight of the symbol, the seer sees its inwardness, things as in a mirror by reflection, and is told that he sees objects reversed as to right and left and must allow for it. Mathers explains the result as part of the seer's nerve and spirit force projected on the symbol, drawing more akashic force to it from around; Brodie-Innes as a tract of the brain made sensitive enough by the symbol to perceive what is pictured on the sphere of sensation, which reflects the whole cosmos and, if time is an illusion, all that was and will be."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XI, Clairvoyance, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 78-79", "Flying Roll XXV, Essay on Clairvoyance and Travelling in the Spirit Vision, by Sub Spe (J. W. Brodie-Innes), ibid., pp. 85-87" .

pmc:GoldenDawnAstralProjection a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Astral projection: scenes seen in three dimensions, from above, and entered as an actor"@en, "Astralprojektion: Szenen dreidimensional, von oben gesehen und als Handelnder betreten"@de ;
    skos:definition "When the ray is emitted and reaches the place, the seer perceives differently from the mirror vision: scenes and things have the third dimension and solidity, stand out first in relief, then are seen as from a balloon; he feels free to descend on the place, step into the scene and act there, and objects are not necessarily reversed. Brodie-Innes, for whom the visions had been no more solid than a kinetoscope, says the power of going to the scenes and seeing them as solid, of doing things and producing effects there, grows out of the sensitivity once established."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XI, Clairvoyance, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 79-80", "Flying Roll XXV, Essay on Clairvoyance and Travelling in the Spirit Vision, by Sub Spe (J. W. Brodie-Innes), ibid., p. 88" .

pmc:GoldenDawnPassageThroughElements a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Passing in projection through every element: flying fast or slow, swimming, going through earth and fire"@en, "In der Projektion durch jedes Element gehen: schnell oder langsam fliegen, schwimmen, durch Erde und Feuer gehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Having succeeded in projection, the Adept is to practise it and, at whatever place he reaches, to try to pass through all the elements; with will, success follows, and practice enables him to fly through the air quickly or slowly as he wills, to swim through water and to pass fearlessly through earth and fire with the aid of the divine names."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XI, Clairvoyance, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 80" .

pmc:GoldenDawnClairaudience a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Clairaudience: a definite sound sent for a definite purpose, rarely a bell"@en, "Hellhören: ein bestimmter Klang zu einem bestimmten Zweck gesandt, selten eine Glocke"@de ;
    skos:definition "The trained ear obtains some success in clairaudience, which is sometimes easier than clairvoyance; those who get some clairvoyance often hear sounds the world does not, frequently a definite sound sent for a definite purpose. Westcott notes that the astral bell of the Theosophists is almost entirely Eastern, and that a Hermetist who hears what others cannot very rarely hears a bell."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XIX, The Aims and Means of Adeptship, by N.O.M. (W. Wynn Westcott), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 117" .

pmc:GoldenDawnMagnetPerception a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Feeling a magnet near the skin with closed eyes, and in time telling its north pole from its south"@en, "Einen Magneten mit geschlossenen Augen nahe der Haut fühlen, und mit der Zeit Nord- von Südpol unterscheiden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The most elementary training of touch: the student will find that with closed eyes he can detect the presence of a magnet held near the skin, best at the forehead, and that with continued practice he can tell the north pole from the south."@en ;
    skos:note "The one capacity in the rolls that names its own test and needs nothing from the tradition to run it: a magnet, a dummy, a second person to hold them and a count. The roll states the outcome as what the student will find, and gives no case. The protocol is at pmc:GoldenDawnMagnetPerceptionProtocol."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XIX, The Aims and Means of Adeptship, by N.O.M. (W. Wynn Westcott), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 117" .

pmc:GoldenDawnSixthAndSeventhSense a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "The sixth sense of astral perception and the seventh of knowledge received from spiritual sources"@en, "Der sechste Sinn der astralen Wahrnehmung und der siebte des Wissens aus geistigen Quellen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Following the Theosophical septenary, Westcott names two senses beyond the five: the sixth, astral perception, the power of perceiving forces and entities on the plane next to the earth, and the seventh, of which he expects some of his hearers to get glimpses in time, the faculty of receiving knowledge from spiritual sources. Neither has an organ, so the existing organs are used."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XIX, The Aims and Means of Adeptship, by N.O.M. (W. Wynn Westcott), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 117" .

pmc:GoldenDawnTarotJudgement a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Judgement by the Tarot: almost anything one wishes for, and results more true than geomancy or astrology"@en, "Urteil durch den Tarot: fast alles, was man wünscht, und wahrere Ergebnisse als Geomantie oder Astrologie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Westcott says the Tarot goes much deeper than the other two systems and gives truer results because its points of contact with the world, with man and his influences are more numerous; with a properly conducted process and a cultivated intuition one can obtain almost anything one wishes for, and once grasped it gives results that are most amazing. Flying Roll XXVIII adds that holding the sword and the matching implement in a Tarot judgement conduces to accuracy."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XIX, The Aims and Means of Adeptship, by N.O.M. (W. Wynn Westcott), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 120", "Flying Roll XXVIII, On the Value of Magic Implements and Insignia in Methods of Divination, by D.D.C.F. and N.O.M., ibid., Appendix D, p. 280" .

pmc:GoldenDawnHoraryPrediction a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "A recovery and its date read from a horary figure: convalescent in four weeks and a day"@en, "Eine Genesung und ihr Datum aus einem Stundenhoroskop gelesen: genesend in vier Wochen und einem Tag"@de ;
    skos:definition "From the figure of 26 February 1887 Berridge judged sure recovery from the acute attack and escape from death, but not a return to vigorous health, and timed the convalescence at four weeks and one day, 27 March. He reports that on 29 March the uncle walked in his garden for the first time and recovered fully from the pneumonia. He adds that a trine or sextile would have made him predict a vigorous old age besides, and that a chronic case would have given only a partial restoration."@en ;
    skos:note "A dated prediction with its outcome, two days off, reported by the predictor, and the only such case in the rolls read. What weakens it is printed beside it: the ten observations each show a rule that, applied differently, would have given another date or another verdict, and each is set aside because the outcome did not match. Read from that side, the observations are a list of the judgements the outcome refuted, and the rule that survived was chosen after the outcome was known; whether the predictor committed to it before 27 March is stated only in his own words. Acute pneumonia, as he says himself, is self-limiting. The protocol is at pmc:GoldenDawnHoraryPredictionProtocol."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XXIV, part of a lecture on horary astrology delivered in 1892, by Resurgam (E. W. Berridge), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), Appendix D, pp. 276-278" .

pmc:GoldenDawnPsychicLink a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "The psychic link that binds the members of the Order to one another"@en, "Das psychische Band, das die Mitglieder des Ordens aneinander bindet"@de ;
    skos:definition "Westcott names as the effect of the salutation the direct maintenance of the psychic link that has always bound the members of the Order one to another. Bullock describes the same from the other side: entering the Second Order brings the student within the radius of a psychic and spiritual force generated centuries ago, with a momentum of its own, which silently exerts a protective influence that the isolated student lacks, and the forces put out by the Golden Dawn ancestors devolve on their successors whether they know it or not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XVI, The History of the Rosicrucian Order, by N.O.M. (W. Wynn Westcott), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 110", "Flying Roll XXVII, The Principia of Theurgia, by L.O. (P. W. Bullock), ibid., pp. 172-173" .

pmc:GoldenDawnHigherGenius a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Union with the Higher Genius: the head under Kether, the consciousness raised from Ruach into Neschamah"@en, "Vereinigung mit dem Höheren Genius: das Haupt unter Kether, das Bewusstsein aus Ruach in Neschamah gehoben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first aim the Obligation names: to purify and exalt the spiritual nature so as to unite oneself with the Higher Genius. Mathers places the genius in Chiah and Yechidah and says it is touched only through the Neschamah, so that the work is to move the ordinary consciousness out of the Ruach; one who can live at will in the Neschamah and touch the genius has gone far toward the elixir of the spirit of life. Moina Mathers gives the same as the head placed immediately under Kether, the beginning of the real initiation, and says the Neophyte reaches it only at very rare moments, for a few seconds, in which the divine vision almost blinds him and passes only partly to the lower selves."@en ;
    skos:note "Mathers adds a failure of the genius itself: having touched the Ruach in one incarnation, it may be so worked on by the sufferings of the lower part that it slackens the union, and if it identifies too much with the Neschamah instead of with the God part, it falls, with some evil effect."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XIX, The Aims and Means of Adeptship, by N.O.M. (W. Wynn Westcott), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 115", "Flying Roll X, Concerning the Symbolism of Self-Sacrifice, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), ibid., pp. 134 and 137-138", "Flying Roll XXI, Know Thyself, by V.N.R. (Moina Mathers), lecture of 24 September 1893, ibid., pp. 151 and 156" .

pmc:GoldenDawnRegenerativeRadiation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "The Adept in contact with his genius gives out the higher force to those ready to receive it"@en, "Der Adept in Verbindung mit seinem Genius gibt die höhere Kraft an die weiter, die bereit sind, sie zu empfangen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Moina Mathers sets the regeneration of the race as an aim of the Order: the Adept whose human will is one with his divine will, and who is therefore always in contact with his genius, can attract still higher forces, and their rays radiate through him to human beings ready to receive them; he is better fitted for this than an angel or a god because he is still a man of Assiah. She illustrates it from Zanoni, whose companions awoke to purer lives without knowing it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XXI, Know Thyself, by V.N.R. (Moina Mathers), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 155-156" .

pmc:GoldenDawnNepheschTransformation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "The highest ray brought down into the body: a Nephesch so transformed that one walks the earth almost like a god"@en, "Der höchste Strahl in den Körper herabgeführt: eine so verwandelte Nephesch, dass man fast wie ein Gott auf Erden geht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mathers says that if the great force of the highest can be made to send its ray down through the Neschamah into the mind and thence into the physical body, the Nephesch would be so transformed as to make one almost like a god walking on the earth; in the fully initiated Adept the Nephesch is so withdrawn into the Ruach that even their lowest parts cease to be allied to the body. He reads the resurrection body of the Gospel, with the solidity of the ordinary body and the faculties of the spirit body, in these terms."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll X, Concerning the Symbolism of Self-Sacrifice, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 136-137" .

pmc:GoldenDawnTransmutationOfMetals a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Transmutation of the base metals into gold and silver by the red and the white elixir"@en, "Umwandlung der unedlen Metalle in Gold und Silber durch das rote und das weiße Elixier"@de ;
    skos:definition "Westcott is entirely convinced that the transmutation of lower metals into gold and silver is possible and has often been done, not by chemistry only but by joining to the physical process the action of the will and the power over the soul of nature and of things, which only purity of life and the Adept's training supply. The alchemist sought the red elixir for gold and the white for silver, chiefly as steps toward the third. The power, so far as he knows, comes beside other magical attainments, is not conferred by any grade, is now and then rediscovered by a private student, is never taught in so many words, and may come when least expected."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll VII, Alchemy, by S.A. (W. Wynn Westcott), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 181-182 and 191" .

pmc:GoldenDawnElixirVitae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "The elixir of life: to intensify, prolong and expand life, so that the Adept loses less time"@en, "Das Lebenselixier: das Leben verstärken, verlängern und ausdehnen, damit der Adept weniger Zeit verliert"@de ;
    skos:definition "The third and superior object of the alchemist, given to vegetable and animal alike to intensify, prolong and expand life. Westcott holds that the two metallic elixirs were sought mainly as steps to it, so that the Adept might lose less time on the way to his goal than in many short lives with passive intervals, and he reads the three chiefs of the Rosy Cross, whose years are counted in hundreds, as allegory of a truth that adeptship links some souls to earthly work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll VII, Alchemy, by S.A. (W. Wynn Westcott), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 182 and 189" .

pmc:GoldenDawnTelesmaCharge a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "A telesma that holds its charge for an indefinite period, and the exhaustion that is the charge passing into it"@en, "Ein Telesma, das seine Ladung unbestimmt lange hält, und die Erschöpfung, die das Übergehen der Ladung ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "A consecrated telesma holds the charge of force more firmly and, carefully kept from contamination and from other telesmata, retains it for an indefinite period. All powerful work of this kind exhausts the vital force, especially in beginners, who will feel distinct exhaustion at first; it is said to be akasha not lost but transferred to the symbol, from which it fades only slowly. In flashing tablets most students can perceive flashes of radiance, partly subjective and partly objective."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XI, Clairvoyance, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 77" .

pmc:GoldenDawnSimulacrum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "The simulacrum of a distant person or place, built by the formative power toward its true conformation"@en, "Das Abbild einer fernen Person oder eines fernen Ortes, von der bildenden Kraft auf seine wahre Gestalt hin gebaut"@de ;
    skos:definition "To picture to oneself persons and things seen is an invocation of their simulacra, and with practice much plastic power of formation develops and the tinge of personality is overcome. The true conformation of the place or being so thought of serves as the archetype on which the formative power builds and to which the image tends naturally to conform, unless preconceptions in the mind impede it. Bullock adds that invocation involves response, on the testimony of psychic sensibility."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XXVII, The Principia of Theurgia, by L.O. (P. W. Bullock), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 171" .

#
# The states the prerequisites point at
#

pmc:GoldenDawnPurity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Purity of body, mind, intellect and emotion, kept in the city and without asceticism"@en, "Reinheit von Körper, Geist, Verstand und Gefühl, in der Stadt bewahrt und ohne Askese"@de ;
    skos:definition "Westcott sets purity before strength of will: the spiritual powers flourish only as the animal soul is starved, while the animal body is kept in health and strength but not petted, and moderation is the rule. The Hermetists, he says in Flying Roll XIX, have always taught that the necessary purity can be combined with the absence of ostentatious morality and unnatural habits, and that one who stays pure in a crowded city earns more than one who buries himself in a wilderness. Bullock names absolute purity and devotion to truth the first condition of theurgia."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll II, part I, A Subject for Contemplation, by N.O.M. (W. Wynn Westcott), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 55", "Flying Roll XIX, The Aims and Means of Adeptship, by N.O.M., ibid., p. 118", "Flying Roll XXVII, The Principia of Theurgia, by L.O. (P. W. Bullock), ibid., p. 170" .

pmc:GoldenDawnEquipoise a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Equipoise: the contrary forces of the body harmonised, the six Sephiroth of the Ruach in order"@en, "Gleichgewicht: die gegensätzlichen Kräfte des Körpers ausgeglichen, die sechs Sephiroth des Ruach in Ordnung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Bullock calls equipoise the circulation of the contrary forces of attraction and repulsion among the centres of sensation, the equal mean between two extremes, and makes it the condition under which the will can be handled at all. Mathers asks for the Mem and the Shin of the nature balanced so that Aleph rises gently between them; Moina Mathers for perfect order in the six Sephiroth of the Ruach, under which the Qlipoth, kept only by disorder, retire and the averse persona becomes a trained animal the man rides."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll II, part II, Remarks upon Subject for Contemplation, by Levavi Oculos (P. W. Bullock), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 57-58", "Flying Roll XI, Clairvoyance, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), ibid., p. 81", "Flying Roll XXI, Know Thyself, by V.N.R. (Moina Mathers), ibid., p. 154" .

pmc:GoldenDawnPositivity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "The positive attitude, the extreme contrast to mediumship"@en, "Die positive Haltung, der äußerste Gegensatz zur Medialität"@de ;
    skos:definition "Westcott: so long as one leads an ordinary life one is safe from influences beyond the material world, but whoever seeks occult mysteries comes under forces he knows little of, and the only way to avoid being controlled by them is the positive attitude, the extreme contrast to mediumship; a medium cultivates negativity and is to be avoided, and the negative attitude both stops progress and exposes one to attack by elemental forces. Bullock carries it past death: unless the soul has learnt in life to be positive to its own astral nature, it is subject after death to the unimpeded fantasy of its own creations."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XIX, The Aims and Means of Adeptship, by N.O.M. (W. Wynn Westcott), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 119-120", "Flying Roll XXVII, The Principia of Theurgia, by L.O. (P. W. Bullock), ibid., p. 169" .

pmc:GoldenDawnWill a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "The trained will: undivided, unmixed with desire, and fearing nothing fate can do"@en, "Der geschulte Wille: ungeteilt, unvermischt mit Begierde, und nichts fürchtend, was das Schicksal tun kann"@de ;
    skos:definition "The rolls distinguish will from desire throughout. One cannot will too strongly, but must not will two things at once nor desire others while willing one; the habit of doing two or three things at once is fatal to the occultist, and the cultivation of an undivided will is to go on at all times. Farr adds the will that fears nothing fate can do, built in oneself as a fortress, and Bullock calls will the grand agent of all occult work, all-powerful over the nervous system, by which the fleeting vision is fixed on the astral light."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll I, part II, A Subject for Contemplation, by N.O.M. (W. Wynn Westcott), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 53", "Flying Roll XIX, The Aims and Means of Adeptship, by N.O.M., ibid., p. 121", "Flying Roll XIII, Secrecy and Hermetic Love, by S.S.D.D. (Florence Farr), ibid., p. 163", "Flying Roll XXVII, The Principia of Theurgia, by L.O. (P. W. Bullock), ibid., p. 172" .

pmc:GoldenDawnCorrespondenceKnowledge a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Thorough knowledge of the correspondences between the forces of the macrocosm and the microcosm"@en, "Gründliche Kenntnis der Entsprechungen zwischen den Kräften des Makrokosmos und des Mikrokosmos"@de ;
    skos:definition "Bullock's second condition of theurgia: a thoroughly trained knowledge of the correspondences between the forces of the greater and the lesser world and of their interaction. Brodie-Innes makes the same knowledge the mechanism of the vision itself, since knowing the attributions of a symbol concentrates thought, nerve force and blood on the matching tract of the brain; Mathers says the tattvas are safe in the hands of a Zelator Adeptus Minor because his knowledge supplies what is wanting in them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XXVII, The Principia of Theurgia, by L.O. (P. W. Bullock), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 170", "Flying Roll XXV, Essay on Clairvoyance, by Sub Spe (J. W. Brodie-Innes), ibid., p. 86", "Flying Roll XXVI, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), ibid., p. 94" .

pmc:GoldenDawnCalm a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "The suitable state: the mind steady and undisturbed, peace, solitude and leisure, health and courage"@en, "Der geeignete Zustand: der Geist ruhig und ungestört, Friede, Alleinsein und Muße, Gesundheit und Mut"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mathers asks for a mind steady, clear and undisturbed, a powerful will, peace, solitude and leisure and every disturbing influence banished, and tells the operator to leave off when the desire of sleep comes and never to force a disinclined mind. Flying Roll XXVIII asks the diviner to be calm, peaceful, healthy and courageous, the mind not wandering, the body at rest, and the personal and social attitude cast aside."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll XI, Clairvoyance, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), pp. 78 and 81", "Flying Roll XXVIII, On the Value of Magic Implements and Insignia in Methods of Divination, by D.D.C.F. and N.O.M., ibid., Appendix D, p. 279" .

#
# The prerequisites
#

pmc:GoldenDawnWillPresupposesPurity a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "Strength of will presupposes purity of body, mind, intellect and emotion"@en, "Willensstärke setzt Reinheit von Körper, Geist, Verstand und Gefühl voraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Westcott puts purity before even strength of will for anyone who hopes for magical power, on the ground that the spiritual powers flourish only in the measure that the animal soul is starved."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:GoldenDawnWill ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:GoldenDawnPurity ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without purity of body, mind, intellect and emotion there is no magical power to hope for: the spiritual powers flourish only as the animal soul is starved." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll II, part I, A Subject for Contemplation, by N.O.M. (W. Wynn Westcott), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 55" .

pmc:GoldenDawnWillPresupposesEquipoise a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "The manipulation of the will presupposes equipoise"@en, "Die Handhabung des Willens setzt Gleichgewicht voraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Bullock calls the condition of equipoise necessary before the manipulation of the will is even possible."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:GoldenDawnWill ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:GoldenDawnEquipoise ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without equipoise the manipulation of the will is not possible at all." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll II, part II, Remarks upon Subject for Contemplation, by Levavi Oculos (P. W. Bullock), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 57" .

pmc:GoldenDawnScarletRayPresupposesKetherRay a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GoldenDawnFlyingRolls ;
    rdfs:label "The scarlet ray in the heart presupposes the ray drawn down from Kether"@en, "Der scharlachrote Strahl im Herzen setzt den aus Kether herabgezogenen Strahl voraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mathers sets the ray from Kether through Gimel into Tiphereth before the scarlet ray is brought into intense action, which makes the effect powerful and the process safer."@en ;
    skos:note "One of the few places in the rolls where a bodily harm is attached to a named omission, with a condition of frequency."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:GoldenDawnScarletRay ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:GoldenDawnKetherRay ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Otherwise there is a risk to the heart and a risk of fever, if the exercise is done frequently." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GoldenDawn ;
    dcterms:source "Flying Roll VI, Concerning Flying Roll No. II, by D.D.C.F. (S. L. MacGregor Mathers), in Francis King (ed.), Astral Projection, Ritual Magic and Alchemy (Rochester, Vermont: Destiny Books, 1987), p. 61" .

# CONTINUED-5
